grass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Turmeric","ingredient/turmeric"],["Anchovy","ingredient/anchovy"],["Bamboo shoot","ingredient/bamboo-shoot"],["Bean curd","ingredient/bean-curd"],["Caraway seed","ingredient/caraway-seed"],["Carrots","ingredient/carrots"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Dried shrimp","ingredient/dried-shrimp"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Leafy greens","ingredient/leafy-greens"],["Long beans","ingredient/long-beans"],["Onions","ingredient/onions"]]}]},"ingredient/salo":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/salo","n":"Salo","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 3 dishes across 5 cuisines","intro":[["Salo or slanina is a European food consisting of salt-cured slabs of pork subcutaneous fat with or without skin and with or without layers of meat. It is commonly eaten and known under different names across Central, Eastern and Southeastern Europe. It is usually dry salt or brine cured. The East Slavic, Hungarian and Romanian variety may also be cured with paprika or other seasonings added, whereas the South and West Slavic version is often smoked."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-salo"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salo_(food)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":5,"groups":[["Hungarian cuisine","cuisine/hungarian-cuisine",[["Rakott krumpli","dish/rakott-krumpli"]]],["Lebanese cuisine","cuisine/lebanese-cuisine",[["Makanek","dish/makanek"]]],["Portuguese cuisine","cuisine/portuguese-cuisine",[["Cocido","dish/cocido"]]],["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine",[["Cocido","dish/cocido"]]],["Ukrainian cuisine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine",[["Rakott krumpli","dish/rakott-krumpli"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Lunch meat","ingredient/lunch-meat"],["Phaseolus vulgaris","ingredient/phaseolus-vulgaris"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Lamb","ingredient/lamb"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Hungarian sausages","ingredient/hungarian-sausages"],["Sour cream","ingredient/sour-cream"]]}]},"dish/digestive-biscuit":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/digestive-biscuit","n":"Digestive biscuit","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from British cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["A digestive biscuit, sometimes described as a sweet-meal biscuit, is a semi-sweet biscuit that originated in Scotland. The digestive was first developed in 1839 by two doctors to aid digestion. The term digestive is derived from the belief, around the time the biscuit was first introduced, that they had antacid properties due to the use of sodium bicarbonate as an ingredient. Historically, some producers used diastatic malt extract to \"digest\" some of the starch that existed in flour prior to baking."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-digestive-biscuit"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digestive_biscuit"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":9,"links":[["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Leavening agent","ingredient/leavening-agent"],["Malic acid","ingredient/malic-acid"],["Malt extract","ingredient/malt-extract"],["Sodium bicarbonate","ingredient/sodium-bicarbonate"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Tartaric acid","ingredient/tartaric-acid"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Wholemeal","ingredient/wholemeal"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Great British Chefs: traditional British recipes","ref":"site/greatbritishchefs-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"greatbritishchefs.com","note":"UK chef-driven recipe site with a large section of classic British dishes.","url":"https://www.greatbritishchefs.com/features/our-most-popular-traditional-british-recipes","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from British cuisine","links":[["68–86 Bar and Restaurant","dish/68-86-bar-and-restaurant"],["Banbury ca